    <h1>ABOUT ELSEWHERE</h1>
    <h2>A card can travel almost anywhere. And sometimes, it can take you elsewhere.</h2>
    <p>There is something romantic and increasingly nostalgic about sending a card from somewhere and finding one waiting at home. A postcard carries an image, a place, a few words and the trace of another person’s moment. However small, it can interrupt the everyday and briefly take us somewhere else.</p>
    <p>The archive emerged out of the artist-run space <a class="external" href="https://endsafter.ch" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">ENDSAFTER ↗</a> and is curated by <a class="external" href="https://ibulla.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">iBulla ↗</a> as an ongoing collection of such moments. Artists and contributors are invited to create a postcard and accompany it with a Registry Note. Together, the cards form editions that connect individual works through a shared format and system of registration.</p>
    <p>Postcards are objects surrounded by formats, postal rules, country codes and international standards — yet what they carry is deeply personal. They can cross borders, change hands, disappear into drawers or remain precious for decades.</p>
    <p>ELSEWHERE ARCHIVE collects these fragments without trying to fix their meaning. It is an archive of images, notes, memories and places — real, imagined or somewhere in between.</p>
    <p>The hope is simple: that the collection keeps growing, that new editions bring new entries, and above all, that the cards leave the archive again — sent to friends, strangers and lovers of art across the world.</p>
    <p><b>ELSEWHERE ARCHIVE</b> begins with the postcard: a small object made to travel.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
